      {
        "id": 38604,
        "name": "Ronway",
        "username": "Ronway",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/{size}/1856_2.png",
        "created_at": "2012-09-18T06:23:22.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"ketigid\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/k/9fc29f/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> ketigid:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This brings up some interesting questions.The original flavour text for Elbow Smash is \"Hah! This is a cute little toy of war!\" -Haka, Battle Unending <span class=\"hashtag-raw\">#8</span>.If it is now changed to \"You are not nearly as fearsome as your namesake.\", then which comic issue is it from? What happens to the old flavour text? If we assume that this 2nd ed is canon, then does the old flavour text cease to exist? Or do they co-exist?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>The flavor text for the new Elbow Smash is still from the same issue interestingly enough. Though I do believe they flavour text probably still exist and if a comic is ever made of that issue both lines would appear in it at some point or another. I think the main reason for switching of flavour text was to give those who actually owned the first edition something else to look foward to and give even more hints to speculating. What fun would exploring the decks be if it was still the same old lines that were already seen a million and two times before hand.</p>",
